<%# The auth sheet's first frame (story 0017 Release 2). No layout.

    ASWebAuthenticationSession can only open a URL, and OmniAuth's request phase
    is POST-only, so something has to turn the one into the other. This page is
    that something: it exists for the handful of milliseconds between the sheet
    opening and the provider's consent screen replacing it.

    Linen and nothing else. A reader on a slow connection sees the product's own
    paper rather than a white flash, and no words — words here would be read as
    a step in the flow rather than the absence of one.

    `<noscript>` carries a real button. The sheet has JavaScript, so this is for
    the case where it does not: better a control than a dead end. %>
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
    <title>Signing in — Tondo</title>
    <%= csrf_meta_tags %>
    <style>
      html, body { height: 100%; margin: 0; background: #f4efe6; }
    </style>
  </head>
  <body>
    <%# `native=1` rides the QUERY STRING, not a hidden field, and that is not a
        style choice. OmniAuth's request phase stashes `request.GET` — only the
        query — into the session as `omniauth.params`, and hands that back on
        the callback. A field in the POST body is never seen again, so the
        callback could not tell the sheet's flow from a browser's. %>
    <%= form_with url: "/auth/#{@provider}?native=1", method: :post, id: "start",
          data: { turbo: false } do |f| %>
      <noscript><%= f.submit "Continue" %></noscript>
    <% end %>
    <script>document.getElementById("start").submit();</script>
  </body>
</html>
